  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Kalashtar are a race of humanoids bound to good-aligned spirits from the\n\tDreamlands, the plane where minds travel to when they sleep. The Kalashtar\n\toriginate in Eberron, seeing their first appearance as a playable race in back\n\tin DnD 3.5 in the Races of Eberron supplement."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Kalashtar’s racial traits center around the mind, including increases to\n\tWisdom and Charisma, Advantage on Wisdom saves, resistance to psychic damage,\n\tand limited telepathy. While these capabilities are powerful, they also\n\tpigeon-hole the Kalashtar into spellcasting classes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "With the custom origin rules in place, the Kalashtar becomes an effective\n\toption for martial classes who are frequently vulnerable to mind-affecting\n\tproblems like spells and special abilities which might frighten or charm them,\n\tas well as to Psychic damage which is rare but basically impossible for most\n\tmartial characters to avoid."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Regardless of whether you’re using the custom origin rules or now, the\n\tKalasthar’s capabilities are almost exclusively defensive. While they do\n\tprovide excellent benefits, they don’t help your character actively do\n\tanything (except potentially sneaking around language barriers with\n\ttelepathy), so be sure that your class features and skills offer enough\n\toptions to make you useful."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
